Drop Cargo.toml and src/main.rs into a folder called depgraph/ (matching that layout), then:

sh
cargo build --release
./target/release/depgraph <input_folder> graph.dot
dot -Tpng graph.dot -o graph.png
How it works: it walks the tree for *.d files, parses each rule (handling \-continued lines and escaped spaces), maps each target/prerequisite path to its first-level subfolder under root, and records an edge target_folder -> prereq_folder when they differ. Same-folder and out-of-tree (e.g. /usr/include) dependencies are dropped silently, per your answer. When both A->B and B->A exist, they're merged into one red dir=both edge instead of two black arrows.

Since I had no Rust toolchain or network in this sandbox, I couldn't cargo build/cargo test it myself — I only checked brace/paren balance by hand. I did include a #[cfg(test)] module covering the tricky bits (continuation lines, escaped spaces, drive-letter colons, folder detection, and the red-edge merge logic) — please run cargo test on your end before trusting it on a real tree, and let me know if anything doesn't compile and I'll fix it.

One thing worth double-checking on your data: if any .d file mixes absolute and relative paths (e.g. system headers found via -I are sometimes relative), the relative ones fall back to being resolved against the .d file's directory, which may not match your actual build directory. Happy to add a --base-dir flag if that turns out to matter.


